      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=6{t}^{2}+12t+6.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}+12t+6 & =0 \\\\ 6(t+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "9c27e36f-7922-5bc6-bc2f-194fb1ebf4b4"
    },
