  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083278,
      "id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}),\\,y=2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "b97c1cb4-2c44-5409-98c1-954974ce94c3"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "2aa3acca-becd-5a28-bc2c-29a074cca7af",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "b97c1cb4-2c44-5409-98c1-954974ce94c3"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "91bbd526-a818-5fc3-9d9b-0334ae515efe"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "10faacdf-f4e4-5dd2-a15b-03ae15f4e772",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "91bbd526-a818-5fc3-9d9b-0334ae515efe"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "d3307f67-abec-5daa-bac0-745c1f4ccf09"
    },
